[{:db/id 17592186228865 :question/problem "Consider the d’Alembert differential equation\n\n$$\ny = x\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}-1\\right)^2 + 2\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right).\n$$\n\nIf $y'(x) = t,$ then general solution can be expressed in parametric form $(x(t), y(t)),$ where\n\n$$\ny(t) = x(t) f(t) + g(t)\n$$\n\nFind $f(t)+g(t).$\n\n*Hint: Under the given transformation, we have*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} - \\dfrac{2(t-1)}{t-(t-1)^2}x = \\dfrac{2}{t-(t-1)^2},\\qquad t-(t-1)^2\\ne0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=y'(x)$ into the original differential equation:\n\n$$y=x(t-1)^2+2t.$$\n\nTherefore $f(t)=(t-1)^2$ and $g(t)=2t$, so\n\n$$f(t)+g(t)=(t-1)^2+2t=t^2+1.$$\n\nFor the nonconstant parameter branch, differentiate with respect to $t$ and use $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$:\n\n$$t x'=(t-1)^2x'+2(t-1)x+2.$$\n\nWriting $D(t)=t-(t-1)^2$ yields the corrected linear equation\n\n$$x'-\\frac{2(t-1)}{D(t)}x=\\frac{2}{D(t)},\\qquad D(t)\\ne0.$$\n\nOn any interval avoiding the roots of $D$, choose a base point $t_0$ and define\n\n$$I(t)=\\exp\\!\\left(\\int_{t_0}^{t}\\frac{-2(s-1)}{D(s)}\\,ds\\right).$$\n\nThen\n\n$$x(t)=\\frac{C+\\int_{t_0}^{t}2I(s)/D(s)\\,ds}{I(t)},\\qquad y(t)=x(t)(t-1)^2+2t.$$\n\nIndeed $(Ix)'=2I/D$, so these formulas satisfy the corrected linear equation and $dy/dt=t\\,dx/dt$. They define a local graph where $x'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nThe excluded constant slopes $r=(3\\pm\\sqrt5)/2$ satisfy $r=(r-1)^2$ and give the separate straight-line solutions $y=rx+2r$. The original elementary expression for $x(t)$ and its associated hint are incorrect for the original equation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
